Patch 48's PC=64 helper takes four arguments and divides 128 by 64 bits
with one divq.

Measured on the Windows build (2026-09-17, a DOS x87 kernel at 64-bit
precision -- fild, fld m32, fdivp, fsqrt, fistp -- under wine against the
Linux build): the Windows vCPU spent 10.8 % in helper_x87x_arith against
4.6 % on Linux, and 4.3 % in libgcc's __udivti3 against 1.75 %. Both are
the Windows x64 ABI: four integer arguments go in registers and a fifth on
the stack behind 32 bytes of shadow space, and __udivti3's 128-bit operands
go through memory. So:

- the two sign | exponent words (32 bits each) go to the helper as one
  64-bit argument, a's high and b's low -- four arguments in all;
- x87f_div_x divides through X87F_UDIV128, which fpu_helper.c defines as
  QEMU's udiv_qrnnd (a divq on x86-64: the dividend's high half is below
  the divisor, so the quotient fits), and x87-fast.h keeps the portable
  unsigned __int128 division for tools/x87-fast-test.c, which compiles
  the header on its own.

The kernel went from 92.5 to 86.0 ns a pass on the Windows build (1.26x
Linux -> 1.17x); unchanged on Linux, where that kernel's time is
floatx80_sqrt's. tools/x87-guest-test.py: 906,713 result lines identical
with the fast path on and off; tools/x87-fast-test.c: 0 failures.

